    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Compact phase-generator mixture",
        "reference": "items/def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-borel-probability-law-on-a-polish-space",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-borel-probability-law-on-a-polish-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Definition\n\nLet $K$ be a nonempty compact subset of $\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, with its Borel sigma-algebra, and let $a:K\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. The **convex hull** $\\operatorname{conv}K$ is the set of finite convex combinations of points of $K$. A **phase generator** is a point $y\\in K$ with declared energy $a(y)$. A **mixture** is a Borel probability measure $P$ on $K$. Its barycenter and mean energy are the coordinatewise Lebesgue integrals\n$$\\bar y(P):=\\int_K y\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad \\bar a(P):=\\int_K a(y)\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R.$$\nThese integrals are finite because each coordinate and $a$ are bounded on compact $K$. For prescribed mean $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$, the **mixture energy** is\n$$a_{\\rm mix}(x):=\\inf\\{\\bar a(P):P\\text{ is a Borel probability on }K,\\ \\bar y(P)=x\\}.$$\nThe admissible set is nonempty since every finite convex combination of points of $K$ is the barycenter of a finite atomic probability. The definition does not assert that the infimum is attained; the theorem on this page gives a compact continuous sufficient condition.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe definition concerns a mathematical probability mixture and an assigned scalar energy. It does not assert that physical phases can be prepared, exchanged, or brought to equilibrium.\n",
        "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy.md.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete author-hosted lecture text",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter read; §§1.2–1.4, finite canonical and microcanonical ensemble conventions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 635-page lecture notes retrieved and read; §§4.2–4.6, pp.192–217, ensembles, microcanonical phase space, quantum density matrices, and traces."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Elastic collision model and weak collision identity",
        "reference": "items/def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-measure",
          "def-measure-space"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-measure-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Definition\n\nFix $d\\ge2$. The **elastic collision set** is\n$$Q_{\\rm el}:=\\{(v,w,v',w')\\in\\mathbb R^{4d}:v+w=v'+w',\\ |v|^2+|w|^2=|v'|^2+|w'|^2\\},$$\nwith its relative Borel sigma-algebra. A collision measure is a nonnegative countably additive measure $\\nu$ on this measurable space. It is symmetric if it is invariant under the exchanges $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(w,v)$, $(v',w')\\leftrightarrow(w',v')$, and $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(v',w')$. These invariances are hypotheses on $\\nu$, not consequences of energy and momentum conservation.\n\nFor a positive function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to(0,\\infty)$, write $Q(f)$ for a collision operator when the following **weak collision identity** holds for each test function $\\varphi$ used:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\varphi(v)Q(f)(v)\\,dv=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(f(v')f(w')-f(v)f(w))(\\varphi(v)+\\varphi(w)-\\varphi(v')-\\varphi(w'))\\,d\\nu,$$\nwith absolute convergence. This identity is the adopted mathematical collision model. Molecular chaos or a microscopic derivation of it is not part of the definition.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe measure's normalization is chosen so $Q(f)$ has units of $f$ per unit time in an application. No specific cross-section or measured collision rate is asserted.\n",
        "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel.md.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Kinetic Theory, Part 2",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/kintheory/two.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 39-page lecture-note part retrieved and read; §2.1, pp.14–16, Liouville phase-space volume; §§2.2–2.3, pp.20–35, Boltzmann equation and H-theorem."
            },
            {
              "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Plasma Physics, Boltzmann H-Theorem",
              "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/plasma/lectures1/node40.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML section read; equations (3.123)–(3.144), collision production, detailed balance, and Maxwellian form."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-finite-site-shell-and-canonical-law",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite-site canonical law and narrow microcanonical shell",
        "reference": "items/def-tdmath-finite-site-shell-and-canonical-law.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-finite-sum",
          "def-natural-logarithm",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-finite-sum": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-natural-logarithm": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Definition\n\nFix a finite alphabet $A=\\{1,\\ldots,q\\}$, $q\\ge1$, and real single-site energies $e_1,\\ldots,e_q$ in a fixed energy unit. At inverse parameter $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$Z_1(\\beta):=\\sum_{j=1}^q e^{-\\beta e_j},\\qquad p_j(\\beta):=e^{-\\beta e_j}/Z_1(\\beta),\\qquad E(\\beta):=\\sum_{j=1}^q p_j(\\beta)e_j.$$\nFor $N\\ge1$, a configuration $i=(i_1,\\ldots,i_N)\\in A^N$ has additive energy $H_N(i):=\\sum_{r=1}^Ne_{i_r}$. Given a width $\\delta_N\\ge0$, the **shell** is\n$$\\Gamma_N(\\beta,\\delta_N):=\\{i\\in A^N:|H_N(i)-NE(\\beta)|\\le\\delta_N\\}.$$\nWhen this finite set is nonempty, its microcanonical law means the uniform probability on $\\Gamma_N$, and its dimensionless entropy is $\\log|\\Gamma_N|$. A type is a vector $t=(n_1/N,\\ldots,n_q/N)$, where the nonnegative integer counts sum to $N$; its multiplicity is $N!/\\prod_j n_j!$.\n\nThe convention fixes both the shell center and the law within the shell. It makes no physical claim that an apparatus has resolution $\\delta_N$.\n",
        "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-finite-site-shell-and-canonical-law.md.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete author-hosted lecture text",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter read; §§1.2–1.4, finite canonical and microcanonical ensemble conventions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 635-page lecture notes retrieved and read; §§4.2–4.6, pp.192–217, ensembles, microcanonical phase space, quantum density matrices, and traces."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Ornstein–Uhlenbeck integral equation",
        "reference": "items/def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-brownian-motion",
          "def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-brownian-motion": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Definition\n\nLet $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,(\\mathcal F_t)_{t\\ge0},P)$ carry a standard Brownian motion $B$ adapted to the filtration, with future increments independent of the present sigma-algebra. Fix constants $\\gamma>0$ and $\\sigma>0$, and an $\\mathcal F_0$-measurable real random variable $X_0$ with finite second moment. A **continuous adapted solution of the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck equation** on $[0,T]$ is a continuous adapted process $X$ satisfying, almost surely for every $t\\in[0,T]$,\n$$X_t=X_0-\\gamma\\int_0^tX_s\\,ds+\\sigma B_t,$$\nwhere the time integral is the ordinary Lebesgue integral along the continuous path. In differential notation this is $dX_t=-\\gamma X_t\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dB_t$, with the stochastic differential interpreted through the displayed integral equation.\n\nThe definition asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. The Itô integral in the explicit representation is the square-integrable predictable-process integral of [[def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes]].\n",
        "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation.md.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "G. A. Pavliotis, Stochastic Processes and Applications",
              "url": "https://www.ma.imperial.ac.uk/~pavl/PavliotisBook.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 155-page author-hosted book retrieved and read; §3.7 explicit Ornstein–Uhlenbeck solution; §§4.1–4.2 transition density and Fokker–Planck equation."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Kinetic Theory, Part 3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/kintheory/three.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 26-page lecture-note part retrieved and read; §§3.1.2–3.2.3, pp.54–70, Langevin dynamics, velocity diffusion, and Fokker–Planck equation."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Regular energy shell and weighted area measure",
        "reference": "items/def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Definition\n\nLet $O\\subset\\mathbb R^m$, $m\\ge2$, be open, let $H\\in C^2(O,\\mathbb R)$, and let $J\\subset\\mathbb R$ be open. An energy $E\\in J$ is **regular** if $\\nabla H(x)\\ne0$ for every $x\\in\\Sigma_E:=H^{-1}(\\{E\\})$. By the implicit-function theorem, a neighborhood of each point of a nonempty regular level is a graph $x_i=h(z)$.\n\nIn a graph chart, use the local area density $\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla h(z)|^2}\\,dz$, where $dz$ is $(m-1)$-dimensional Lebesgue measure. A **graph partition** on $\\Sigma_E$ is a locally finite graph-chart cover with nonnegative smooth functions $\\chi_a$ supported in their respective charts and summing to one on the level. Whenever such a partition is supplied, define the candidate weighted shell integral by\n$$\\mu_E(g):=\\sum_a\\int g(z,h_a(z))\\chi_a(z,h_a(z))|\\partial_iH(z,h_a(z))|^{-1}\\,dz$$\nfor nonnegative Borel $g$. Its independence from the chosen chart partition, and its equality with $\\int_{\\Sigma_E}g|\\nabla H|^{-1}dA_E$, are proved in the theorem on this page. If $0<\\mu_E(1)<\\infty$, the **microcanonical shell probability** is $\\widehat\\mu_E:=\\mu_E/\\mu_E(1)$.\n\nThis is an explicit chart-based definition, not a formal delta distribution $\\delta(H-E)$. Critical levels are excluded.\n",
        "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell.md.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete author-hosted lecture text",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S2",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter read; ideal-gas phase-space integrals and canonical Gaussian momentum distribution."
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 635-page lecture notes retrieved and read; §§4.2–4.6, pp.192–217, ensembles, microcanonical phase space, quantum density matrices, and traces."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-continuous-ideal-gas-block-limit",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Continuous ideal-gas bulk limit and finite-block bridge",
        "reference": "items/thm-tdmath-continuous-ideal-gas-block-limit.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "cor-integral-logarithm-is-strictly-increasing",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "def-natural-logarithm",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
          "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "thm-gaussian-integral",
          "thm-integration-by-parts",
          "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "thm-of-square-roots",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "cor-integral-logarithm-is-strictly-increasing": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-natural-logarithm": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gaussian-integral": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-integration-by-parts": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Statement\n\nFix dimension $d\\ge1$, particle mass $m>0$, action reference $h>0$, inverse energy $\\beta>0$, and positive cube volumes $V_N$ with $N/V_N\\to\\rho\\in(0,\\infty)$. For $N$ indistinguishable classical noninteracting particles in a cube of volume $V_N$, use the phase-space reference $dq\\,dp/(N!h^{dN})$ and Hamiltonian $H_N(q,p)=\\sum_{i=1}^N|p_i|^2/(2m)$. Define $\\lambda_\\beta=h\\sqrt{\\beta/(2\\pi m)}$. Then the dimensionless partition integral and free-energy limits are\n$$Z_N=\\frac{V_N^N}{N!\\lambda_\\beta^{dN}},\\quad\\frac{-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z_N}{N}\\longrightarrow\\beta^{-1}[\\log(\\rho\\lambda_\\beta^d)-1],\\quad\\frac{-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z_N}{V_N}\\longrightarrow\\beta^{-1}\\rho[\\log(\\rho\\lambda_\\beta^d)-1].$$\nThere is also the following finite-momentum-block limit. Let $M=dN$, let $G_1,\\ldots,G_M$ be independent standard normal variables, set $A_N=M^{-1}\\sum_{a=1}^MG_a^2$, and define canonical momenta $P_a^{\\rm can}=\\sqrt{m/\\beta}\\,G_a$ and shell-prepared momenta $P_a^{\\rm sh}=\\sqrt{m/\\beta}\\,G_a/\\sqrt{A_N}$. The shell-prepared vector has exactly energy $dN/(2\\beta)$, and for every fixed $r$ and bounded continuous $\\psi:\\mathbb R^r\\to\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\mathbb E\\psi(P_1^{\\rm sh},\\ldots,P_r^{\\rm sh})\\longrightarrow\\mathbb E\\psi(P_1^{\\rm can},\\ldots,P_r^{\\rm can}).$$\nThis is weak convergence for each fixed block in this free-particle model. The shell preparation is the stated normalized-Gaussian construction, and no finite-width shell or interacting-gas equivalence is asserted.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** The free-particle Hamiltonian, phase-space normalization, and normalized-Gaussian shell preparation.\n\n[A1] $d,N\\ge1$, $m,h,\\beta,V_N>0$, $N/V_N\\to\\rho\\in(0,\\infty)$, and the $N$-particle Hamiltonian is additive kinetic energy in a cubic position box.\n\n[A2] For the block limit, the $G_a$ are independent with density $(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/2}$ and $r$ is fixed while $N\\to\\infty$.\n\n[F1] [[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]] and [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]: the phase-space integrals are Lebesgue integrals, and integrability/Fubini hypotheses are stated for the displayed densities.\n\n[F2] [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] and [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]: finite products of the Lebesgue factors carry the rectangular product measure.\n\n[F3] [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] and [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]: nonnegative product integrals factor by Tonelli; integrable products may be evaluated iteratively.\n\n[F4] [[thm-gaussian-integral]]: $\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-x^2}\\,dx=\\sqrt\\pi$.\n\n[F5] [[thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions]] and [[thm-of-square-roots]]: the scaling substitution $u=\\sqrt c\\,x$ is valid for $c>0$, so $\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-cx^2}\\,dx=\\sqrt{\\pi/c}$.\n\n[F6] [[def-natural-logarithm]], [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]], and [[cor-integral-logarithm-is-strictly-increasing]]: the library natural logarithm equals the integral logarithm and is strictly increasing on positive reals.\n\n[F7] [[thm-integration-by-parts]]: on each finite interval, differentiable factors with integrable derivatives obey the ordinary integration-by-parts identity.\n\n[F8] [[thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial]]: $R^k e^{-R^2/2}\\to0$ for each fixed nonnegative integer $k$, by setting $x=R^2/2$ and bounding $x^{k/2}$ by a larger integer power.\n\n## Proof\n\n\n\n**Given:** [A1] and, for the second conclusion, [A2].\n\n1.1 The integrand is nonnegative, so [F3] factors the position and momentum integrals: $$Z_N=\\frac1{N!h^{dN}}\\int_{\\Lambda_N^N}\\int_{\\mathbb R^{dN}} e^{-\\beta\\sum_i|p_i|^2/(2m)}\\,dp\\,dq =\\frac{V_N^N}{N!h^{dN}}\\left(\\int_\\mathbb R e^{-\\beta p^2/(2m)}\\,dp\\right)^{dN}.$$ By [F4]–[F5] the one-coordinate integral is $\\sqrt{2\\pi m/\\beta}$, proving the stated exact formula with $\\lambda_\\beta=h\\sqrt{\\beta/(2\\pi m)}$. The dimensions are consistent: $\\lambda_\\beta$ is a length, so $V_N^N/\\lambda_\\beta^{dN}$ and $Z_N$ are dimensionless. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5]\n\n1.2 Let $G$ have the density in [A2]. By [F4]–[F5], it is normalized. Apply [F7] on $[-R,R]$ first with $u(x)=x$, $v'(x)=xe^{-x^2/2}$, and then with $u(x)=x^3$, $v'(x)=xe^{-x^2/2}$. In each case $v(x)=-e^{-x^2/2}$. Letting $R\\to\\infty$, the endpoint terms vanish by [F8], giving $\\mathbb E G^2=1$ and $\\mathbb E G^4=3$. Thus $\\mathbb E(G^2-1)=0$ and $\\mathbb E(G^2-1)^2=2$. Product measure and [F3] make distinct coordinates independent, so expanding the square and factoring cross integrals gives $$\\mathbb E(A_N-1)^2=M^{-2}\\sum_{a=1}^M2=2/M.$$ For any $\\eta>0$, $\\eta^2\\mathbf1_{\\{|A_N-1|>\\eta\\}}\\le(A_N-1)^2$, so integration proves $P(|A_N-1|>\\eta)\\le2/(M\\eta^2)\\to0$. Hence $A_N\\to1$ in probability. [A2, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, F8, algebra]\n\n2.1 Monotonicity of $\\log$ from [F6] gives $\\int_1^N\\log x\\,dx\\le\\log N!\\le\\int_1^{N+1}\\log x\\,dx$, hence $\\log N!=N\\log N-N+O(\\log(N+1))$. Substituting the exact $Z_N$ yields $$N^{-1}\\log Z_N=\\log(V_N/N)+1-d\\log\\lambda_\\beta+o(1)\\to-\\log\\rho+1-d\\log\\lambda_\\beta.$$ Multiplication by $-\\beta^{-1}$ proves the per-particle limit; multiplying by $N/V_N\\to\\rho$ proves the per-volume limit. [A1, F6, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 Since $A_N>0$ almost surely, the shell-prepared variables are defined. Algebra gives $$\\sum_{a=1}^{dN}\\frac{(P_a^{\\rm sh})^2}{2m} =\\frac1{2\\beta A_N}\\sum_aG_a^2=\\frac{M}{2\\beta}=\\frac{dN}{2\\beta}.$$ This exact energy constraint holds on every outcome where $A_N>0$, an event of probability one since the joint Gaussian density assigns zero mass to the origin. [A2, step 1.2]\n\n2.3 For fixed $r$, the scalar multiplier $A_N^{-1/2}$ tends to $1$ in probability by continuity at $1$ and step 1.2. Therefore $(P_1^{\\rm sh},\\ldots,P_r^{\\rm sh})-(P_1^{\\rm can},\\ldots,P_r^{\\rm can})$ tends to zero in probability: restrict first to the event $\\sum_{a=1}^rG_a^2\\le R^2$, where its norm is at most $\\sqrt{m/\\beta}R|A_N^{-1/2}-1|$, and then bound the complement by $P(\\sum_{a=1}^rG_a^2>R^2)\\le r/R^2$ using the second moment. For bounded continuous $\\psi$, choose $R$ so this tail is small, use uniform continuity of $\\psi$ on the compact ball of radius $2\\sqrt{m/\\beta}R$, and then use convergence in probability of the difference. On the complement the expectation difference is at most $2\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty$. Sending first $N\\to\\infty$ and then $R\\to\\infty$ proves the claimed expectation limit. [A2, F1, F3, step 1.2]\n\n3.1 If positions are included, choose them independently and uniformly in the same cube under both preparations. After coordinatewise rescaling by the side length, every fixed position block already has the same uniform law in both models and is independent of the momentum vector. The same bounded-continuous test argument as in 2.3, first on compact sets and then on their small tails, proves the joint fixed-block limit. This does not change the energy computation because the Hamiltonian has no position dependence. [A1, A2, F1, step 2.3] ∎\n",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/thm-tdmath-continuous-ideal-gas-block-limit.md and its proof contract.",
