          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-measure-preserving-transformation-and-system": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-probability-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-birkhoff-ergodic-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-integrals-are-invariant-under-measure-preserving-maps": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-liouville-volume-preservation": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $(M,\\omega)$ be a smooth $2n$-dimensional symplectic manifold and $H\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$, with Hamiltonian field defined by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$. Wherever its local flow $\\phi_t$ is defined, it preserves the symplectic form and the volume measure $m$ determined by $\\omega^n/n!$. No completeness is asserted. For a region $R$ on which a nonzero-time map $T=\\phi_\\tau$ and all its iterates are defined, $T$ is a measure-preserving transformation. If $m(R)<\\infty$, then for every measurable $E\\subseteq R$, almost every point of $E$ returns to $E$ infinitely often under forward iteration.\n\nIf $m(R)=1$ and $T$ is ergodic, then for every $f\\in L^1(R,m)$ the averages $N^{-1}\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}f(T^jx)$ converge almost everywhere and in $L^1$ to $\\int_Rf\\,dm$. Without ergodicity, the limit is the invariant conditional expectation supplied by Birkhoff's theorem, not necessarily the space mean. Finally, if each stated time map $\\phi_t$ is a diffeomorphism of a finite invariant region $R$, a density $p_0\\ge0$ with $\\int_Rp_0\\,dm=1$ and $\\int_R|p_0\\log p_0|\\,dm<\\infty$ transports by $p_t=p_0\\circ\\phi_{-t}$ with conserved fine-grained entropy $-k_B\\int_Rp_t\\log p_t\\,dm$, using $0\\log0=0$. Volume preservation alone does not imply ergodicity or a time-average/ensemble identity.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** The symplectic/Hamiltonian definitions, the local flow domain, and each separately stated finite invariant probability system.\n\n[A1] AC is adopted; $(M,\\omega)$ is smooth symplectic of dimension $2n$, $H$ is smooth, and $X_H$ follows $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$.\n\n[A2] The local flow is used only where defined. For entropy transport, each stated $\\phi_t$ is a diffeomorphism of $R$ with inverse $\\phi_{-t}$. For recurrence and Birkhoff, $R$ is invariant, $T=\\phi_\\tau$ and every iterate exist on $R$, and $m(R)<\\infty$; for the space-mean conclusion $m(R)=1$ and $T$ is ergodic in the sense of [F5].\n\n[A3] The transported density is measurable, nonnegative, normalized, and has finite absolute entropy integral, with $0\\log0=0$.\n\n[F1] [[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold]], [[def-hamiltonian-vector-field-and-hamiltonian-function]], and [[cor-symplectic-manifolds-have-a-canonical-orientation-and-volume-form]]: AC is the inherited measure-theorem convention; $\\omega$ is closed and nondegenerate, $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$, and $\\omega^n/n!$ determines the canonical positive volume measure.\n\n[F2] [[thm-liouville-volume-preservation]]: every local Hamiltonian flow map preserves this symplectic volume on the domain where the map is defined.\n\n[F3] [[def-measure-preserving-transformation-and-system]] and [[thm-integrals-are-invariant-under-measure-preserving-maps]]: the stated invariant flow map is measure preserving, and integrals of integrable functions are unchanged by its measure-preserving substitution.\n\n[F4] [[thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems]]: on a finite-measure system, almost every point of each measurable set returns to that set infinitely often.\n\n[F5] [[def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system]], [[thm-birkhoff-ergodic-theorem]], and [[cor-birkhoff-ergodic-theorem-for-ergodic-probability-systems]]: ergodicity means every invariant event is null or conull; for an integrable observable on a finite measure-preserving probability system, time averages converge almost everywhere and in $L^1$; the ergodic case has constant limit equal to the space integral.\n\n[F6] [[def-probability-measure]] and [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]: normalized densities and the stated absolute-integrability condition define the probability and entropy integrals.\n\n## Proof\n\n\n\n**Given:** [A1]–[A3] and the inspected supplier interfaces [F1]–[F6].\n\n1.1 By [F1], $\\omega$ is closed and $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$. The Hamiltonian-flow theorem [F2], under its local existence domain, gives $\\phi_t^*\\omega=\\omega$ and hence $\\phi_t^*(\\omega^n/n!)=\\omega^n/n!$. The volume measure defined by this positive form is therefore preserved on each invariant domain; restriction to $R$ gives the measure-preserving system of [F3]. Also $dH(X_H)=\\omega(X_H,X_H)=0$ because a two-form is alternating, so each defined orbit stays on its energy level. This argument grants no flow outside its domain. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 Apply [F3] to $p_t=p_0\\circ\\phi_{-t}$. It preserves $\\int p_t\\,dm=\\int p_0\\,dm=1$. The scalar function $u\\mapsto u\\log u$, defined to be $0$ at $u=0$ by [A3], is Borel; its absolute integrability is preserved because the same measure-preserving substitution gives $$\\int_R|p_t\\log p_t|\\,dm=\\int_R|p_0\\log p_0|\\,dm<\\infty.$$ Applying integral invariance to its positive and negative parts proves $\\int_Rp_t\\log p_t\\,dm=\\int_Rp_0\\log p_0\\,dm$, hence the stated fine-grained entropy conservation. [A2, A3, F3, F6, step 1.1]\n\n2.2 The map $T$ on $R$ is measure preserving by 1.1 and [A2]. Its total measure is finite, so [F4] gives infinite returns for almost every point in each measurable $E\\subseteq R$. The conclusion is explicitly almost everywhere and applies to the sampled map at the specified $\\tau$. [A2, F3, F4, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 Under the additional probability and ergodicity assumptions in [A2], the hypotheses of [F5] hold for each $f\\in L^1(R,m)$. Birkhoff therefore gives almost-everywhere and $L^1$ convergence to the invariant conditional expectation, and its ergodic corollary identifies that limit with $\\int_R f\\,dm$. If ergodicity is removed, only the invariant conditional expectation conclusion remains. Neither [F2] nor recurrence [F4] supplies ergodicity. [A2, F5, step 1.1] ∎\n",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/thm-tdmath-hamiltonian-measure-and-recurrence.md and its proof contract.",
