    "items": [
      {
        "id": "cex-tdmath-noncompact-mixture-nonattainment",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Noncompact mixtures can have an unattained energy infimum",
        "reference": "items/cex-tdmath-noncompact-mixture-nonattainment.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy",
          "thm-tdmath-compact-continuous-mixture-attainment"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-compact-continuous-mixture-attainment": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Statement\n\nExtend the objective in [[def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy]] to the noncompact Borel generator set $K=(0,1)$ with energy $a(x)=x$, and impose no barycenter constraint. The infimum of $\\int_Kx\\,dP(x)$ over Borel probability measures is $0$, but no probability measure attains it. Thus compactness and the stated feasibility conditions in [[thm-tdmath-compact-continuous-mixture-attainment]] cannot be discarded in a general attainment claim.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** The explicit noncompact set, continuous energy, and unconstrained Borel-probability objective.\n\n[A1] $K=(0,1)$, $a(x)=x$, and $P$ ranges over all Borel probability measures on $K$ with objective $\\int_Kx\\,dP$.\n\n[F1] [[def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy]]: mixture energy is the integral of the assigned generator energy against a probability measure.\n\n[F2] [[thm-tdmath-compact-continuous-mixture-attainment]]: compactness of the generator space is among the hypotheses of its attained-minimum result.\n\n## Proof\n\n\n\n**Given:** [A1] and the mixture objective [F1].\n\n1.1 For each integer $n\\ge2$, the set $E_n=[1/n,1)$ is Borel, and $\\bigcup_{n\\ge2}E_n=(0,1)$. Since $P((0,1))=1$, countable subadditivity implies $P(E_n)>0$ for at least one $n$; otherwise the union would have probability zero. On $E_n$, $x\\ge1/n$, while elsewhere $x\\ge0$, so $$\\int_{(0,1)}x\\,dP\\ge \\frac1nP(E_n)>0.$$ Therefore every admissible probability has strictly positive objective. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 The point mass $\\delta_{1/n}$ at $1/n$ is a Borel probability on $(0,1)$ and has objective $1/n$. These values tend to zero, while 1.1 shows all objectives are positive. Hence the infimum is zero and is not attained. The generator set is noncompact, so this conclusion does not contradict the compact result [F2]; it shows why an attainment theorem must retain its compactness/coercivity hypotheses. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1] ∎\n",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/cex-tdmath-noncompact-mixture-nonattainment.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete author-hosted lecture text",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter read; §§1.2–1.4, finite canonical and microcanonical ensemble conventions."
            },
