      {
        "id": "cex-tdmath-nonergodic-volume-flow",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A finite-volume Hamiltonian map that is not ergodic",
        "reference": "items/cex-tdmath-nonergodic-volume-flow.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system",
          "def-hamiltonian-vector-field-and-hamiltonian-function",
          "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold",
          "thm-tdmath-hamiltonian-measure-and-recurrence"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-hamiltonian-vector-field-and-hamiltonian-function": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-hamiltonian-measure-and-recurrence": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "\n## Statement\n\nOn the symplectic torus $\\mathbb T^2=(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^2$ with $\\omega=dq\\wedge dp$, let $H(q,p)=\\cos q$. The Hamiltonian flow is complete and preserves finite area, but every nonzero-time map is nonergodic: the invariant set $A=\\{(q,p):\\cos q>0\\}$ has exactly half the total area. This gives a concrete reason volume preservation alone cannot identify time averages with whole-space ensemble averages.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** The symplectic torus, canonical Hamiltonian convention, and area measure.\n\n[A1] $q,p$ are angular coordinates modulo $2\\pi$, $\\omega=dq\\wedge dp$, and $H(q,p)=\\cos q$.\n\n[F1] [[def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold]] and [[def-hamiltonian-vector-field-and-hamiltonian-function]]: a Hamiltonian vector field satisfies $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$.\n\n[F2] [[thm-tdmath-hamiltonian-measure-and-recurrence]]: a complete invariant Hamiltonian flow map preserves symplectic area.\n\n[F3] [[def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system]]: ergodicity requires every invariant measurable set to have measure zero or full measure.\n\n## Proof\n\n\n\n**Given:** [A1] and the canonical convention [F1].\n\n1.1 Write $X_H=a\\partial_q+b\\partial_p$. Then $\\iota_{X_H}(dq\\wedge dp)=a\\,dp-b\\,dq$, while $dH=-\\sin q\\,dq$. Equating coefficients gives $a=0$ and $b=\\sin q$. The explicit solution is $$\\phi_t(q,p)=(q,p+t\\sin q\\pmod{2\\pi}),$$ which is defined for every real $t$ and is a diffeomorphism with inverse $\\phi_{-t}$. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Since $H\\circ\\phi_t=H$, the set $A$ is invariant under every $\\phi_t$. The torus has total area $(2\\pi)^2=4\\pi^2$. The allowed $q$ values in $A$ have total length $\\pi$, while $p$ ranges over length $2\\pi$, so $m(A)=2\\pi^2$, strictly between zero and total area. The map preserves area by [F2], and the invariant set violates the criterion [F3]; hence it is not ergodic for any nonzero sampling time. [A1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/cex-tdmath-nonergodic-volume-flow.md and its proof contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Kinetic Theory, Part 2",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/kintheory/two.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 39-page lecture-note part retrieved and read; §2.1, pp.14–16, Liouville phase-space volume; §§2.2–2.3, pp.20–35, Boltzmann equation and H-theorem."
            },
